text=['# P0211 literature and source verification','', 'Search date: 2026-10-11. No paper checked below was found to state and prove the relaxation-order question for this exact chain with the required uniform quantifiers. This is a bounded literature check, not an assertion of absence from all literature. The result in ATTEMPT.md is an original derivation.','', '## Primary source','', 'David Aldous, Mixing times for coagulation-fragmentation processes, author-maintained open-problem page (first posted August 2012; page update February 2018): https://www.stat.berkeley.edu/~aldous/Research/OP/coag_frag.html . Linked notes: A special aggregation-fragmentation process, section 1 of coag_frag_mix.pdf, PDF pp. 1-4 (printed page labels 2-5). Copies and extracted text are saved here.','', 'Exact hypotheses: integer partitions of n, pairwise constant merger rate 2/n, per-internal-link fragmentation rate beta>0; reversible mass proportional to (beta*n)^number_of_clusters/product m_j!. Proposition 1 is a fixed-beta n-to-infinity statement. The concluding remark also considers beta_n~b/n, with b>0, and suggests order sqrt(n).','', 'Proposition 1 verbatim (mathematical symbols normalized from PDF extraction): "For the aggregation-fragmentation chain (M(t)) specified above, (1-o(1))(beta^2+4beta)^(-1/2) <= tau_2 <= beta^(-1) as n -> infinity." This is a bound, not the requested sharp order in the small-beta joint limit.','', 'Page-4 remark verbatim: "Our arguments continue to apply in the setting where beta = beta_n ~ b/n, say, though here the upper and lower bounds on tau_2 have orders n and n^(1/2)." The subsequent sentence suggests order n^(1/2) and improved coupling.','', '## Relevant references and exclusions','']
